# GETGRENT(3)

## NAME

getgrent, getgrgid, getgrnam, setgrent, endgrent - get group file entry

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <grp.h>

struct group *getgrent(void);

struct group *getgrgid(int gid);

struct group *getgrnam(const char *name);

void setgrent(void);

void endgrent(void);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getgrent, getgrgid* and *getgrnam* each return pointers to an object with the following
structure containing the broken-out fields of a line in the group file.

> ```c
> struct group {
>     char  *gr_name;
>     char  *gr_passwd;
>     int    gr_gid;
>     char **gr_mem;
> };
> ```

**Note:** v7's page pulled this in with a **.so** of the installed header. It is written out
here, because there is no installed /usr/include on this system - the one header tree is
share/besm6/include. <grp.h> declares all five routines too; v7's declared the struct alone,
leaving the rest to K&R's implicit **int**.

The members of this structure are:

gr_name
: | The name of the group.

gr_passwd
: | The encrypted password of the group.

gr_gid
: | The numerical group-ID.

gr_mem
: Null-terminated vector of pointers to the individual member names.

*Getgrent* simply reads the next line while *getgrgid* and *getgrnam* search until a matching
*gid* or *name* is found (or until EOF is encountered). Each routine picks up where the others
leave off so successive calls may be used to search the entire file.

A call to *setgrent* has the effect of rewinding the group file to allow repeated searches.
*Endgrent* may be called to close the group file when processing is complete.

## FILES

| /etc/group
| share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/stdio/getgrent.c, getgrnam.c, getgrgid.c

## SEE ALSO

getlogin(3), getpwent(3), group(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

A null pointer (0) is returned on EOF or error.

## BUGS

All information is contained in a static area so it must be copied if it is to be saved. **The
struct, the strings and the member vector are all overwritten by the next call**.

## BESM-6 NOTES

The line buffer is **GRLINE**, not BUFSIZ, for the reason getpwent(3) gives: BUFSIZ is 3072
here and describes a disk block rather than a line of text.

One v7 bug is fixed rather than carried, and it is in the member list - the one field
/etc/passwd has no counterpart to. v7 sized the *gr_mem* vector at MAXGRP and then **filled it
without checking,** so a group line with more members than that walked off the end of a
file-scope array into whatever bss followed it. The walk stops with room for the terminator
now, and the tail is dropped. A 512-byte line cannot hold a hundred names in any case; the
bound is the array's, not the format's.
